          "content": "**Learning objective:** Investigate fibers, films, coatings and cellular plastics using draw, orientation, continuity and expansion models.\n\n## What this unit requires\n\nProduct-formation models constrain the amount of polymer per length, area or volume. They do not independently establish microstructure, anisotropy or fracture resistance. A reliable design compares conservation predictions with distributed geometry measurements.\n\nAn engineering statement is useful only after its units, system boundary, assumptions and evidentiary status are defined. This unit deliberately distinguishes mathematical identity, conditional model prediction, laboratory observation and verified process claim. Calculated values in these pages are synthetic worked examples, not measured commercial-resin data. Seek grade- and application-specific property evidence before any equipment or product decision.\n\n## Four-lesson pathway\n\n1. [Melt spinning and fiber draw ratio](../lessons/unit-05-lesson-01.html) \u2014 [numerical model 13](../labs/lab-13.html)\n2. [Film casting, biaxial stretching and orientation](../lessons/unit-05-lesson-02.html) \u2014 [numerical model 14](../labs/lab-14.html)\n3. [Coating thickness and drying mass balance](../lessons/unit-05-lesson-03.html) \u2014 [numerical model 15](../labs/lab-15.html)\n4. [Foaming, expansion and cellular structure](../lessons/unit-05-lesson-04.html) \u2014 [numerical model 15](../labs/lab-15.html)\n\n## Demonstrate learning\n\n- [Sixteen multi-part assessment questions](../assessments/unit-05.html): numerical reconstruction, equation audit, model challenge and design trade-off.\n- [Unit-level engineering project](../projects/unit-05.html): a practical but fictional application with traceable mass, quality and evidence requirements.\n- [Interactive offline laboratory manual](../LABORATORY-MANUAL.html): adjustable, source-visible calculations with CSV export.\n\n## Completion standard\n\nExplain a material mechanism, reconstruct the featured calculation with consistent dimensions, test one limiting case and specify an experiment that could invalidate the conclusion. An equation that reproduces its own arithmetic has been software-verified; it has not thereby been validated against polymer specimens or factory operation."
