          "id": "sec-5-6",
          "secNumber": "5.6",
          "heading": "Process Simulation & Industrial Design Case: Injection Molding and Die Forming Processes",
          "content": "# Unit 05 applied engineering design study \u2014 Product formation\n\n**Case status:** fictional scenario. All measurements, market costs, process coefficients and material specifications are synthetic unless properly sourced by the learner. No calculated output authorizes a machine setting, product certification or environmental claim.\n\n## Case brief\n\nA packaging producer chooses between a biaxially oriented film, coated film and cellular foam for different barrier and stiffness objectives. Each sales claim is supported by one average thickness or density only.\n\nThe essential discipline is to prevent a model from silently becoming evidence. A design team should draw a boundary, define a function, preserve material and energy units, and identify a measurement capable of changing its conclusion. In polymer systems the same nominal resin can behave differently after drying, heating, oxidation, stretching and reprocessing. Product acceptance must be assessed on the relevant final article and sampling plan.\n\n## Technical analyses\n\nReconstruct geometric stretch, coating dry-mass thickness and expansion ratio, then identify orientation, residual solvent, cell structure and anisotropic performance as missing evidence.\n\nDescribe where the physical state changes, where material is lost, where energy crosses the boundary and where quality is gained or degraded. If two alternatives differ by material type or final function, normalize them before comparing. State which inputs are experimentally measured, independently sourced, nominal manufacturer data or assumed illustrative coefficients. Use an uncertainty interval for any decision-critical property and do not invent a measured value.\n\n## Three interactive investigations\n\n1. [Simulation 13](../labs/lab-13.html): sweep the first parameter and reconstruct the default numerical output from its equation; record an analytical limit.\n2. [Simulation 14](../labs/lab-14.html): assess which second physical mechanism the model adds; state whether both calculations use the same material state.\n3. [Simulation 15](../labs/lab-15.html): deliberately test parameter sensitivity or an invalid-domain warning and explain why treating a fictitious curve as a specification would be unsafe.\n\nCalculate at least one output independently in a spreadsheet or short code segment, save the CSV curves, and annotate differences between numerical rounding and structural model assumptions. The three simulations may complement one another without forming a complete virtual factory.\n\n## Evidence protocol\n\nDesign a measurement schedule with sensor or analytical method, instrument range, calibration date, specimen preparation, sampling interval and acceptable data completeness. Define a control sample and a replicate strategy. For process comparisons, separate startup and steady production and keep separate numerator/denominator definitions for material yield, rejected-part rate and energy use. A small systematic bias can outweigh an impressive-looking simulated benefit.\n\nPropose at least two counterhypotheses that could explain the same visible process defect or laboratory trend. Specify the smallest experiment that can discriminate them. State which claim you would withdraw if an independent material or product test contradicts the predicted model curve. When the claim concerns safety, food contact, biodegradation or a regulated end use, require the relevant complete, current standard and qualified expert review.\n\n## Deliverables\n\nDeliver unit-aware geometry calculations, three offline lab sweeps, targeted experimental test methods and a rejection criterion when surface or internal defects become unacceptable.\n\nIn addition, submit a one-page management summary: intended function and specification, explicit assumptions, normalized numerical comparison, evidence gaps, the chosen next experiment and the decision that cannot yet be made. Append all formulas, spreadsheet inputs and raw exports so another person can reconstruct the case. A recommendation is not complete until it specifies what new evidence would change it.\n\n## Assessment rubric\n\n- 25% correctness of mass, dimensional and mathematical reasoning.\n- 20% physically appropriate model scope and identification of omissions.\n- 20% reproducible model sweeps and independent numerical checks.\n- 20% design of experiments and measurement-system integrity.\n- 15% appropriate conditional decision and transparent communication."
